  "name": "Question 33",
  "problem": "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x+2,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 & -3 & 2 \\\\  &  & -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-2} & =-{x}^{2}+x-1 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-x+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-2)({x}^{2}-x+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}-x+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1)=-3<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}-3(0)+2=2\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14110945/assets/f542cc71eb968746be3ca9318c37751ec7486d2b15c002cfe85f3dbd956c4ebc.png)",
